ational midterm environment or Republican consolidation behind Collins could narrow the gap, but the current polling breadth makes a tossup unlikely.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Jon Ossoff (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Georgia's Senate race pits incumbent Democrat Jon Ossoff against Republican Mike Collins. Ossoff has built a dominant financial position, raising $77 million and holding $42.6 million on hand, while Collins has raised $7.4 million with about $2.1 million available. Polling across a dozen surveys shows Ossoff leading by roughly 6 to 11 points, including from Republican-leaning pollsters, with recent averages clustering around 7 to 9 points.\n\nRaters overwhelmingly call the race Likely D, and prediction markets price Ossoff's win probability around 93 to 96 percent. Headlines describe Republican concern that the seat is slipping away, with the NRSC chair leaving Georgia off its target list and even some GOP figures endorsing Ossoff. The race remains a Likely Democratic hold, with the main uncertainty being the size of the margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9499, "margin": 8.78}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9449, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9229, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9348, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9317, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9355, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9397, "margin": 8.5}]}